[2026-10-21T18:17:35.364Z] [safeoutputs] recv: {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"add_comment","arguments":{"body":"Thanks for the report! This is an automated first-pass triage; a maintainer will make the final call.\n\n**Reproduction:** Black 25.11.0, default flags: reformatted, same diff as in the report (the ternary is split over three lines). Black 26.1.0: same output. Black's default safe mode passed, which means it found the result AST-equivalent to the input.\n\n**Assessment:** The change only adds line breaks. In Python, a conditional expression has lower precedence than `==`, so `my_var == 999 if abc == \"TEST\" else 0` already parses as `(my_var == 999) if abc == \"TEST\" else 0`. When `abc == \"TEST\"` this gives `assert my_var == 999`, and otherwise `assert 0`. Both the original and the formatted code mean this. The behaviour described as \"Original code\" would need the parenthesised form `my_var == (999 if abc == \"TEST\" else 0)`. Black does not add those parentheses, because it never changes the meaning of code. See \"Line length\" and \"Parentheses\" in the Black code style docs (black.readthedocs.io).\n\n**Next step:** If you see different runtime behaviour before and after formatting, please share a runnable example that shows it. Otherwise this looks like intended behaviour.\n\n<sub>black-issue-triage skill v1.0.0 · suggested for maintainers: `R: not a bug`</sub>"}}}
